#1 Boundaries and roadmap

#2 Shipped in 1.1

- Lexer, block and inline parsers, and an AST.
- UTF-16, original-source, and UTF-8 byte offset mappings.
- Diagnostics, registries, custom directives, and attribute validation.
- HTML rendering, AST serialization, and structured tables.
- automated core regression tests.
- The official `.sm` extension, `&` directives, short aliases, attributes, diagnostic codes, and the V1.1 compatibility rules.
- YAML 1.2, BibTeX/CSL JSON data APIs, opt-in KaTeX, numbering, the documented Markdown common subset, the formatter, workspace validation, performance, and fuzz tests.

#2 Deliberately outside 1.1

StructMark is not a programming language: there are no variables, conditionals, loops, or macros. Version 1.1 also does not include an editor, cloud collaboration, or a database; full CommonMark/GFM equivalence, a full CSL style engine, and complete LaTeX schema validation are outside the core promise. Those boundaries keep the grammar predictable and let renderers evolve independently.

#2 Next directions

Language-server support, more renderers, document indexing, packaged registries, and a fuller tooling ecosystem will follow real usage rather than assumptions.

#2 v1.2–v1.3 tools now available

The language core has a CLI for checking, converting, and validating `.sm` workspaces plus an AST Visitor API. Playground offers example presets and AST JSON, while the docs can be searched locally. These are tooling improvements, not changes to the stable v1.1 syntax contract.

#2 Standards outside this release

Full CSL styles, LaTeX macros, complete CommonMark/GFM compatibility and incremental parsing remain out of scope. The documented subsets should not be mistaken for full standards compliance.

#2 v1.5 tooling improvements

Workspace indexing now exposes referring-file diagnostics and reference targets. Playground offers a collapsible AST Tree with source-range selection. CLI file replacement and strict conversion are safer. A lossless AST writer, incremental parsing, and full CSL remain outside this release.
